1
00:00:00,138 --> 00:00:02,075
Feed a matrix every arrow on a

2
00:00:02,075 --> 00:00:04,900
circle. However messy the matrix,

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:07,212
out comes a clean ellipse. Why?

4
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:09,970
Take A with rows six, eight and

5
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:11,958
minus four, three.

6
00:00:11,958 --> 00:00:14,971
The two axis arrows land on its columns.

7
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:16,979
Those images are no longer

8
00:00:16,979 --> 00:00:17,805
perpendicular:

9
00:00:18,155 --> 00:00:19,954
their dot product is forty eight

10
00:00:19,954 --> 00:00:22,154
minus twelve, thirty six.

11
00:00:22,742 --> 00:00:23,979
Now turn the pair.

12
00:00:23,979 --> 00:00:26,479
A quarter turn swaps their roles,

13
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:28,517
so the dot product flips to minus

14
00:00:28,517 --> 00:00:29,467
thirty six.

15
00:00:30,067 --> 00:00:31,479
So somewhere in between it is

16
00:00:31,479 --> 00:00:33,942
zero, for any matrix at all.

17
00:00:33,942 --> 00:00:36,042
Here that happens at v one equals

18
00:00:36,042 --> 00:00:37,954
point six, point eight.

19
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:41,642
Their images, ten, zero and zero,

20
00:00:41,642 --> 00:00:44,105
five, are perpendicular too:

21
00:00:44,105 --> 00:00:46,404
the long and short axes of the ellipse.

22
00:00:46,987 --> 00:00:48,875
Any arrow on the circle is a mix

23
00:00:48,875 --> 00:00:51,962
of v one and v two, so its image

24
00:00:51,962 --> 00:00:54,363
is the same mix of the two axes.

25
00:00:54,363 --> 00:00:55,787
That traces an ellipse.

26
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,062
Ten and five are the singular

27
00:00:58,062 --> 00:01:00,563
values, and in matrix form this is

28
00:01:00,563 --> 00:01:02,525
the singular value decomposition:

29
00:01:02,912 --> 00:01:05,675
A equals U Sigma V transpose.
